{
  "abstract": "We study a private $K_7$ whose vertices each have four outside neighbors in a six-terminal set, so every private vertex has total degree ten. Encoding the seven missed terminal pairs as a multigraph gives an exact Hall characterization of nonextendible nine-color palettes when no terminal is common to all private neighborhoods. A safe terminal has at most one forbidden value after the other five terminal colors are fixed. This yields absorption of one safe terminal of exterior degree at most seven, and of an induced three-vertex path of safe terminals each of exterior degree at most eight. If every terminal is missed at least twice, we prove an ambient biplanar reduction by component stars that preserves all exterior vertices and edges, including when a private layer is disconnected. For the balanced missing-pair profile $(01,01,02,13,23,23,45)$, with actual edge $45$, connected private layers, and active terminal sets $\\{0,1,4,5\\}$ and $\\{2,3,4,5\\}$, we construct a six-auxiliary replacement valid in every such drawing. Six auxiliaries are optimal within the specified private-surface routing scope. A hand contact-coherence argument and four certified geometric exclusions give a complete boundary classification. Compact standard-library checkers regenerate the finite domains, validate exact Kuratowski subdivisions and propositional refutations, and verify relative embeddings and coloring relations. These are conditional local reductions; they neither force a private clique to occur nor settle the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem.",
